<sn><md>### Bus Topology

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** All devices connect to a `single central backbone cable`.
- **Data Flow:** Broadcast — signals travel `both directions`; terminators at each end absorb signals.
- **Pros:** Cheap, simple, requires `less cable`.
- **Cons:** If backbone fails, entire network fails; collisions common; hard to troubleshoot.
- **Example:** Early Ethernet (10Base2).
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://www.cablify.ca/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/Bus-Topology-1.jpg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Star Topology

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcRLAaThXUy5Da1RVPPBLzN-VqhJMweGnXbFb7HhPO835g&amp;s=10)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** All devices connect to a `central hub/switch`.
- **Data Flow:** Devices `send data to the central device`, which forwards it.
- **Pros:** Easy to add/remove devices; one cable failure doesn't affect others; easy troubleshooting.
- **Cons:** If `central device fails`, whole network goes down; more cable needed.
- **Example:** Most modern home/office networks.
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Ring Topology

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** Devices connected in a `closed loop`; each connects to `two neighbors`.
- **Data Flow:** Data travels in `one direction` (or both in dual-ring), passing through each node.
- **Pros:** No collisions; predictable performance; `equal access`.
- **Cons:** `One break` can disrupt the whole ring (unless dual-ring); adding devices disrupts network.
- **Example:** Token Ring, FDDI.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://www.cablify.ca/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/Ring-Topology-1.jpg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Mesh Topology

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcQFHtLCUyexpA8gpFyrGPXO8V93chXjosp19-Uuoi6DmBoBf1IFmnEh4hc&amp;s=10)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** Every device connects to every other device.
- **Types:**
  - **Full Mesh:** `Every node linked to every other` (*n(n-1)/2* links).
  - **Partial Mesh:** Some nodes fully linked, others partially.
- **Data Flow:** Multiple paths; data can be `rerouted if a link fails`.
- **Pros:** Highly redundant, reliable, secure.
- **Cons:** Expensive, complex wiring, hard to manage.
- **Example:** Internet backbone, military networks.
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Tree (Hierarchical)

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** Star networks connected to a central backbone in a hierarchy.
- **Data Flow:** Data moves up/down the hierarchy.
- **Pros:** Scalable; easy to expand; good for large organizations.
- **Cons:** If root/backbone fails, whole tree fails; depends on central points.
- **Example:** Large corporate networks, cable TV systems.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://www.cablify.ca/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/Tree-Topology-1.jpg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Hybrid Topology

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://www.cablify.ca/wp-content/uploads/2018/04/Hybrid-Topology.jpg)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** Combination of `two or more topologies`.
- **Data Flow:** Depends on `combined topologies`.
- **Pros:** Flexible, scalable, reliable.
- **Cons:** Complex, expensive to design and maintain.
- **Example:** Star-Bus, Star-Ring (used in many enterprise networks).
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Point-to-Point Topology

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** Direct link `between two devices`.
- **Data Flow:** Dedicated link.
- **Pros:** Simple, fast, secure.
- **Cons:** Not scalable; `limited` to two devices.
- **Example:** Two routers connected via a leased line.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcR2-4ttYvGmpKGi0WMwbBpdDqmTe21OzeExY3vPma88L-iNRtyLEpLr5FB0&amp;s=10)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Point-to-Multipoint Topology

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://docs.digi.com/resources/documentation/digidocs/90001456-13/resources/images/rf_kits/communication_models_2_500x250.png)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** `One central device connects to multiple endpoints`.
- **Data Flow:** Central device shares link with many receivers.
- **Pros:** Efficient use of medium; good for broadcasting.
- **Cons:** Shared bandwidth; central device is a `bottleneck`.
- **Example:** Wi-Fi access point, satellite links.
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Wireless Mesh Topology

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
- **Structure:** Like `mesh` but wireless; *nodes* act as `routers`.
- **Data Flow:** Data hops node-to-node to reach destination.
- **Pros:** Self-healing, no cabling, wide coverage.
- **Cons:** Interference, latency with many hops.
- **Example:** Smart home devices, city-wide Wi-Fi.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://www.techtarget.com/rms/onlineImages/networking-wireless_mesh_mobile.jpg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Quick Comparison Table

| Topology | Cost | Reliability | Scalability | Cable Needed | Fault Isolation |
|----------|------|-------------|-------------|--------------|-----------------|
| Bus | Low | Low | Low | Low | Poor |
| Star | Medium | Medium | High | High | Good |
| Ring | Medium | Medium | Low | Medium | Poor |
| Mesh | High | Very High | Medium | Very High | Excellent |
| Tree | Medium | Medium | High | Medium | Good |
| Hybrid | High | High | High | Varies | Good |
| Point-to-Point | Low | High | Low | Low | Excellent |
</md></sn><sn><md>### Key Takeaways
- **Physical topology** = actual wiring layout.
- **Logical topology** = how data actually flows (can differ from physical).
- **Star and hybrid** dominate modern networks.
- **Mesh** is used where reliability is critical (backbones, military).
- **Bus and ring** are mostly legacy but important for exams and theory.

Choosing a topology depends on **cost, reliability, scalability, and fault tolerance** requirements.</md></sn>